      "output": {
        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to put every change to agreed requirement text through the same review and agreement before committing it. It names two cases: a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]` covers this and carries the {test} marker for the requirement.\n- Its `assert_phrases` call checks the bundled skill text, collapsed so line wrapping does not matter. The skill must contain the sentence \"Every change to agreed text, including a rewording for precision or removing a requirement, goes through steps 3 to 5 before it is committed\". That sentence names both listed cases. It also sets the timing as before the commit. `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` (Workflow section) contains it.\n- `step_of(..., \"Sort the new and changed requirements into groups\")` must equal 3. This ties \"steps 3 to 5\" to the start of the review sequence. If the steps were renumbered, the reference would no longer point at the review. The test file has other tests that pin the order of the steps, and this requirement does not need to cover them.\n- The assertions on the workflow and description wording cover the neighbouring parent requirement. They are not needed for this obligation, but they do no harm.\n\nI found no plausible violation that these tests would accept. If the skill dropped the sentence, narrowed it to some changes, or left out the rewording or removal case, `assert_phrases` would fail. If the numbering drifted, the `step_of` assertion would fail. The test calls `assert_text`, which does a real `contains` check, so the assertions are enforced."
      }
    }
